  "sections": [
    {
      "line": 169,
      "text": "\\section{Introduction}%\\label{INTRO}"
    },
    {
      "line": 304,
      "text": "\\section{Categorical completions}\\label{CATCOMP}"
    },
    {
      "line": 385,
      "text": "\\section[The case of U\\_q(g)]{The case of $\\boldsymbol{U_q({\\mathfrak g})}$}\\label{Uq}"
    },
    {
      "line": 387,
      "text": "\\subsection{Notations}"
    },
    {
      "line": 431,
      "text": "\\subsection{Categorical completions} \\label{catcomb} By a theorem of Harish-Chandra, the map $\\iota_U\\colon U\\to{\\hat U}$ of Section~\\ref{CATCOMP} is injective when $U$ is $U({\\mathfrak g})$, the universal enveloping algebra of~${\\mathfrak g}$ \\cite[Theorem 2.5.7]{Dix}. It is also injective when $k={\\mathbb C}(q)$ and $U=U_q$ \\cite[Lemma~7.1.9]{Jos}."
    },
    {
      "line": 465,
      "text": "\\subsection{Integral forms and specializations}"
    },
    {
      "line": 522,
      "text": "\\subsection{Quantum coordinate algebra}\\label{OQ} Let $U$ be a Hopf algebra over a field $k$, $X$ an object of $\\operatorname{FinMod}_U$, and $\\pi_X$ the representation of~$U$ associated to~$X$. For every $v\\in X$ and $w\\in X^*$ we denote by ${}_X\\phi{}^w_v\\colon U \\ra k$ the linear form defined by"
    },
    {
      "line": 545,
      "text": "\\subsection{Integral quantum coordinate algebra} Finally, we will use the integral form ${\\mathcal O}_A$ of~$\\Oq$ introduced in~\\cite{Lusztig2}, and further studied in \\cite{Lusztig} and \\cite{DC-L} (see \\cite[Remark 4.1]{DC-L} for the equivalence of the two definitions). One defines~${\\mathcal O}_A$ as the $A$-module generated by the set of matrix coefficients of the $U_A^{\\rm res}$-modules of type $1$. Clearly, it is contained in~$\\Oq$; in fact, ${\\mathcal O}_A$ is the subset of~$\\Oq$ formed by the linear maps $f\\colon U_q\\ra {\\mathbb C}(q)$ such that $f(U_A^{\\rm res}) \\subset \\mc\\big[q,q^{-1}\\big]$ (see~\\cite[Section~7]{Lusztig2}). Equivalently one can define~${\\mathcal O}_A$ as the set of $A$-linear maps"
    },
    {
      "line": 555,
      "text": "\\section[The loop algebra L\\_\\{0,1\\}(g)]{The loop algebra $\\boldsymbol{\\Ll_{0,1}(\\mathfrak g)}$}%\\label{loopalg}"
    },
    {
      "line": 784,
      "text": "\\section[The example of L\\_\\{0,1\\}(sl(2))]{The example of $\\boldsymbol{\\Ll_{0,1}({\\mathfrak{sl}}(2))}$}\\label{sl2}"
    },
    {
      "line": 928,
      "text": "\\section[The ''daisy'' graph algebra L\\_\\{0,n\\}(g)]{The ``daisy'' graph algebra $\\boldsymbol{\\Ll_{0,n}(\\mathfrak g)}$}\\label{Lgnalg}"
    },
    {
      "line": 930,
      "text": "\\subsection{Definition and first properties} We define $\\Ll_{0,n}$ as a {\\it twisted product} of $n$ copies of the $U_q\\otimes U_q^{\\rm cop}$-module algebra $\\Ll_{0,1}$ (see Proposition~\\ref{L01surq}), following \\cite{DKM} (but using right modules instead of left ones). Since the twist uses $R$-matrices, as for $\\Ll_{0,1}$ the construction uses as well categorical completions. Eventually we will see that $\\Ll_{0,n}$ is also the braided tensor product of $n$ copies of the $U_q$-module algebra~$\\Ll_{0,1}$ (see, e.g.,~\\cite{Majid} for the notion of braided tensor product)."
    },
    {
      "line": 1076,
      "text": "\\subsection{The Alekseev map} We have defined $\\Ll_{0,n}$ as a twist of $\\Ll_{0,1}^{\\otimes n}$ in Proposition \\ref{defiL0n}, and obtained a presentation by generators and relations in Proposition \\ref{matrelL0n}. Although this presentation seems complicated, the Alekseev map, defined below, identifies $\\Ll_{0,n}$ as a module subalgebra of $\\tilde U_q^{\\otimes n}$."
    },
    {
      "line": 1422,
      "text": "\\subsection{Integral form} Let $\\{\\alpha_i\\}_{i\\in I}$ be a basis of the free $A$-module $\\Ll_{0,1}^A$. Put $(\\alpha_i)^{(a)} := \\mathfrak{i}_a(\\alpha_i)$, where as usual $\\mathfrak{i}_a\\colon \\Ll_{0,1}^A\\ra \\big(\\Ll_{0,1}^A\\big)^{\\otimes n}$ is the inclusion map, $1\\leq a\\leq n$. A basis of the free $A$-module $\\big(\\Ll_{0,1}^A\\big)^{\\otimes n}$ is given by the elements $\\alpha_{i_1\\cdots i_n} = (\\alpha_{i_1})^{(1)} \\otimes \\dots \\otimes(\\alpha_{i_n})^{(n)}$, the products in $\\Ll_{0,n}^A$ of the elements $(\\alpha_{i_1})^{(1)},\\dots, (\\alpha_{i_n})^{(n)}$ (in this order from $1$ to $n$), indexed by the tuples $(i_1,\\dots,i_n)\\in I^n$. For every basis elements $\\alpha_{i_1\\cdots i_n}$, $\\alpha_{j_1\\cdots j_n}$ we have"
    },
    {
      "line": 1465,
      "text": "\\subsection[Localization when g=sl(2)]"
    },
    {
      "line": 1566,
      "text": "\\subsection{Invariant elements} We go back to the general situation of $\\Ll_{0,n}^A = \\Ll_{0,n}^A(\\mathfrak{g})$ for an arbitrary $\\mathfrak{g}$. We need the following fact, proved by Majid \\cite{Majid} for $\\Oq$. For completeness we recall the main ideas of the proof given in \\cite{DKM}, which extends immediately to $\\mathcal{O}_A$."
    },
    {
      "line": 1699,
      "text": "\\subsection{Centers}"
    },
    {
      "line": 1792,
      "text": "\\subsection{Specializations}"
    },
    {
      "line": 1835,
      "text": "\\section[Center of L\\_\\{0,n\\}\\textasciicircum{}{e}(sl(2)) and quantum coadjoint action]"
    },
    {
      "line": 1839,
      "text": "\\subsection[Center of L\\_\\{0,n\\}\\textasciicircum{}{e}(sl(2))]"
    },
    {
      "line": 2167,
      "text": "\\subsection[Gg-invariant central elements and SL(2,C)-characters]"
    },
    {
      "line": 2171,
      "text": "\\subsubsection[The quantum coadjoint action for U\\_e]{The quantum coadjoint action for $\\boldsymbol{U_\\e}$}"
    },
    {
      "line": 2593,
      "text": "\\subsubsection[Extension to L\\_\\{{0,n}\\}\\textasciicircum{}e]"
    },
    {
      "line": 2774,
      "text": "\\section{Topological formulation}\\label{SKEIN}"
    },
    {
      "line": 2776,
      "text": "\\subsection{The Wilson loop functor}\\label{Wfunctor} Recall that we denote by $\\mathcal{C}_A$ the category of $U_A^{\\rm res}$-modules of type $1$, and that $\\mathcal{C}_A \\otimes {\\mathbb C}\\big[q^{1/D},q^{-1/D}\\big]$ is a ribbon category."
    },
    {
      "line": 2930,
      "text": "\\subsection{The Wilson loop isomorphism}\\label{Wiso} One can adjoin to $\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathcal{C}_{A}}(\\Sigma)$ the $A$-span of closed oriented ribbon graphs in $\\Sigma \\times [0,1]$ colored over~$\\mathcal{C}_{A}$ {\\it and possibly having coupons} (see \\cite{Tu} for this notion). Denote the resulting set by $\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathcal{C}_{A}}(\\Sigma)^c$. It is an $A$-algebra for the stacking product, containing $\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathcal{C}_{A}}(\\Sigma)$ as a subalgebra. Define a {\\it one-coupon multicurve} as an element of $\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathcal{C}_{A}}(\\Sigma)^c$ that can be represented by a (oriented, $\\mathcal{C}_A$-colored) ribbon graph embedded in $\\Sigma\\times \\{0\\}$ (whence edges are unlinked and unknotted) and having a single coupon. An example of one-coupon multicurve with the coupon colored by $a_X$ is shown in Figure~\\ref{fig8.3}, where $\\Sigma$ is the four-holed sphere (i.e., $n=3$)."
    },
    {
      "line": 3076,
      "text": "\\subsection[The threading map and G-invariant central elements]{The threading map and $\\boldsymbol{\\Gg}$-invariant central elements}"
    },
    {
      "line": 3164,
      "text": "\\section{Applications to skein algebras}\\label{appskein}"
    },
    {
      "line": 3197,
      "text": "\\subsection*{Acknowledgements} We thank our colleagues of the work group on moduli spaces at IMAG for discussions, especially Paul-Emile Paradan and Damien Calaque. We also thank Pavel~Etingof, Matthieu Faitg, Charles~Frohman, and Catherine~Meusburger for their interest and exchanges related to the present work."
    }
